00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
hi good to see you thanks for joining us  um what are we making of what we're  hearing about the bank of England saying  they're not going to offer any more  support to pensions after Friday are we  worried I think it is worrying there's  obviously been a level of volatility  again in the markets and people are  obviously worried about the cost of  borrowing continuing to increase at  least of all mortgage payers who are  struggling with very high payments at  the moment so I think it is is clearly  worrying but all of this is a response  to the fact that the bank of England has  had to step in three times now to try  and stabilize the markets after the mini  budget the quickest and simplest way to  deal with this is for this government  that has crashed the economy and loaded  the costs onto working people to reverse  the mini budget and provide some calm  and stability in the markets again  yesterday I spoke to the Deputy Prime  Minister she said that the economy was  on track  um and then I spoke to the business  secretary today and he says there's  nothing to worry about yeah I mean we've  got the spectacle of a business  secretary touring the TV studios this  morning trying to convince people that  Pension funds are safe this is a really  serious situation and a very bad  situation for the government to have got  themselves into this is a crisis that  was made in Downing Street it was a  deliberate decision to announce 43  billion pounds worth of unfunded  commitments not to publish an  independent assessment alongside that as  is normally the case it spooked the  markets it's pushed borrowing costs up  it's had a major impact on families  across the country and businesses  particularly mortgage payers who've seen  their mortgages Rise by an average of  500 pounds every month as they come off  fix their mortgages the government needs  to go back to the drawing board  urgently and start again but the  business secretary is saying as far as  Pension funds are concerned they're safe  as houses feel forgive upon but if they  do go under because they're taking to a  higher risk well it  all of this is caused by the government  and there's every attempt that I've seen  on your showcase yesterday with Therese  coffee and today with Jacob Reese mogg  to blame other people just doesn't stick  in the end that shock to the system came  directly after the budget announcement  we've continued to see that instability  and it's a British problem it's not a  global problem it's a it's a problem for  British families who are paying much  higher mortgage costs and the government  needs to go back to the drawing board  start again properly fund their  commitment stop undermining the  independence of independent institutions  that help to bring calm and stability to  the markets and stop asking working  people to pay the price for a crisis  that they've created there isn't enough  money I think we all agree on that so  there isn't enough money for nurses paid  increases well there's a a whole set of  problems here the first is that we've  had a decade of very sluggish growth  we've had the slowest growth in the G7  post pandemic and that's a consequence  of not having invested which is part of  the reason why at our conference we set  our plans to invest in clean energy and  infrastructure and get this country  working again but we've also got this  problem of an economy that has been  crushed by the government so we've got  soaring inflation rates and that's  putting pressure on people's pay packets  as well the best way that you can deal  with this and the way that a labor  government would deal with this is to  scrap this mini budget properly fund and  cost all our commitments which we've  done but the government hasn't respect  the independence of Institutions like  the bank of England and the office for  Budget responsibility which are there to  bring stability and calm to the markets  and not push up the cost of borrowing  and and then go out and get money back  into people's pockets through good jobs  and wages but that's it that's the  that's the alternative that is off for  Britain right now and that's why yes  yesterday kirstylmer said he was putting  the labor party on an election footing  not because there's a lot at stake for  labor but because there's so much at  stake for the country I want to ask you  about the election in just a second but  you know the mini budget is about 45  billion we're already 60 billion in the  whole that's going to get even worse  there's no more money for pay Rises well  the part of the reason there's no money  for pay Rises is because the cost of  borrowing has gone up and the government  is now spending more to service debt  that's why we've said the first thing  that they've got to do before they make  any other commitments is go back to the  drawing board scrap this mini budget and  start again and properly fund and cost  their commitments that's what we're  going to do we haven't gone out making  commitments that this country can't  afford because it's people's money they  haven't got a lot of it and we're going  to be careful with every single penny  and would there be enough for pay Rises  for nurses well I've been absolutely  clear with you on this show before and  I'll continue to be clear that this  country needs a pay rise and when we  clapped for nurses and carers during the  pandemic we meant it but what I'm not  going to do is sit in this studio and  try to conduct some kind of pay  negotiation the government hasn't opened  the books to us we don't know what the  state of the NHS finances are but I can  tell you that when we're in government  if people trust us to to take the reins  and to try and sort this mess out what  we'll do is sit down with our Workforce  and negotiate our way through this to  treat them as partners in British  Endeavor not as enemies to be attacked  and undermined what do you mean by an  election footing well we when kiss  number was first elected a couple of  years ago he said that the first task of  his Premiership was to make labor fit to  govern again and we did that we tackled  anti-Semitism we've dealt with a lot of  the internal problems in the party then  he said to us we've got to go out and be  retro people we're ready to govern with  a plan for Britain and that's what this  conference was all about setting out big  plans for clean energy homegrown energy  more security more money in people's  pockets what he said yesterday is we've  got to get on an election footing  because an election could come at any  time this is the most unstable  government that seen the mood in  Parliament amongstorey MPS is pretty  mutinous we have no idea when a general  election is going to come but it could  come at any moment and we've got to be  ready because there's so much at stake  for this country happy Bonkers to call  an election night I mean you know I I  would suggest you that there would be  Bonkers to announce 43 billion pounds  worth of unfunded spending commitments  and refused to publish an independent  impact assessment of that and then  double down over the course of their  conference series of u-turns but  doubling down on the idea that you can  just keep spending money you don't have  so I genuinely I think this government  could do anything at any moment it's the  most unstable government I think of my  lifetime and so we've got to be ready  uh let me take you back to the governor  of the bank of England and what he has  been saying we've heard both from  quasiquarting and indeed from the Prime  Minister during the uh election campaign  for the leader they were both critical  of Andrew Bailey  um when we had Ruth Morgan um a little  bit earlier he was tiptoeing through the  raindrops what does labor think about it  I'm not going to criticize the governor  of the bank of England because we've  seen what happens when you attack and  undermine the independence of  Institutions that are independent for a  reason they're there to provide calm and  stability and what we've seen in the  last few weeks is the extraordinary  spectacle of the bank of England having  to step in three times to deal with a  government that has crashed the economy  and manufactured a crisis that working  people are paying for what the governor  of the bank of England is clearly trying  to do is bring some calm and stability  back to the market and to shore up the  bond market so that we don't see Pension  funds collapsing he needs to be given  space to get on with that but what we  most need to see is a government that  recognizes the mess that they've made  goes back to the drawing board and  starts again he says he's had lots of  sleepless nights of late Andrew Bailey  what about you  um a lot yeah I mean a couple of reasons  why one is that posts the budget my  office like most MPS offices was flooded  with people particularly mortgage payers  people saying the housing costs going up  as well as the food as well as their  energy prices and just not knowing how  they're going to survive this winter but  also because I know how much is at stake  that we have got to go out and show  people that we can be trusted to govern  again I think people are starting to  believe that again I would never have  thought that three years ago when we had  our worst election defeat in 100 years  but there's so much at stake for this  country and we've got to go out and make  sure that we we offer the change that is  needed your leader will be at the  dispatch box I'm guessing a little bit  later on facing Liz truss one wonders um  what um tack he might adopt might he  mentioned the Arsenal again in his  speech I have absolutely no idea what  he's going to do today what I do know is  that he's he's really concerned about  what happening to families up and down  this country and the biggest thing that  we want the government to do is to scrap  this mini budget and go back to the  drawing board so I suspect you'll see a  lot of that from Kirsten later but I  suspect as well that you might see quite  a bit of it from the Tory bat benches  we'll see thank you have you got any  intel on that and well just that I've  been in Parliament for yesterday after  the conference recess and I think there  is a very somber mood amongst  conservative MPS particularly you know  you've got the Richie soon act  supporters who feel very much like a  party within a party at the moment  they're very unhappy about the direction  of travel they don't seem to know what  to do about it we'll be calling on Tory  MPS to really stand up for the interests  of the country we all know that they've  got to scrap this mini budget we know  that they've got to start again we know  they've got to deal with working people  shouldering most of the costs for those  decisions and I hope that we can  persuade a lot of those story MPS to  stand up for the interests of Britain  and do the right thing okay it's good to  see you as always listen Andy thank you  very much indeed thanks

